the doctor and the boyfriend here we go  again the caretaker this is probably the  episode of the new series that I'm the  most simple about it it's not bad I  don't want to give that impression but  I've got the most mixed feelings about  out of anything so far largely just  because what is dealing with is an  aspect of current version of Doctor Who  that I really don't care for and never  have I call it the domestics because  that's what the doctor himself is called  and I'm talking about the things that  tie a companion back to the real world  so parents siblings friends boyfriends  whatever it's something that the show  never dealt with in the classic era and  in that regard is one of the things that  I prefer the classic eras approach  because when the new show first started  dealing with that it was kind of  interesting because why as I said  classic era number did it but for me it  got old really quick and they just keep  doing it again and again and again  Rosa's family Martha's family Donna's  family  Amy drags along Rory Clara got double  dipped for her own family and the family  that she was watching the kids for it's  just never an aspect I found  particularly interesting and here we go  again as Clara gets in deeper with her  relationship with Danny and obviously  that conflicts with her life on the  TARDIS with the doctor that said it  wasn't terrible I wasn't in pain it's  one of those things were like okay if we  have to do this this is about as well as  it's gonna get done largely because I  still like the character of Danny pink  himself and that does take the curse off  it a bit I like him as a character unto  himself I like his interactions with  Clara I actually ended up really liking  his interactions with the doctor which I  wasn't sure I was gonna like because  they've gone very heavy-handed with sort  of setting up what the two of them  meeting is gonna be like and by that I'm  referring to all the stuff that's gone  on in episodes up to this point  referring repeatedly to the fact that  the doctor doesn't like soldiers now  here's the thing if you've been watching  the show really ever you should kind of  know that already and even if you don't  just one or two reminders is enough but  this current series has really just been  hammering that point over and over again  it's like okay fine we get it and it was  so obvious why they were doing it to set  up for when he inevitably runs into  Danny that there was going to  that tension in that conflict but the  thing is those two characters butt heads  naturally enough that it didn't need  this big setup and that's sort of the  other thing that takes the curse off it  as ham-fisted as the set up before the  conflict was you get the two of them in  a room together I like the way the  interact I like the way they bounce off  each other I like the way they challenge  each other another thing that I I'm  surprised that I liked is the fact that  these sort of sci-fi more classic Doctor  Who aspect of the story is really kind  of sidelined its background for the most  part and normally that would annoy me  but they've they've scaled the story  such that it works very well it's a such  a simple straightforward story that  there would have been no point in  spending the entire episode on it it's  too simple but as the through line as  something to keep the doctor doing and  keep him busy it works very well Peter  Capaldi is still doing great work he  also has some interesting things with  with one of the students at the school  that he's pretending to be the caretaker  at and I like that enough that I'm  actually kind of torn as to whether I  want to see them do anything more with  that or not I liked what went on I'd be  interested in seeing again but then  again as a one-off it felt it felt like  a completed moment it didn't feel like  something worth revisiting and oddly I  actually liked Clara quite a bit I feel  like we got a better definition on her  in this 